ual and some age better than  others and in different ways so that  would be my recommendation I'm trying I  believe I'm a reasonably well-rounded  player I'm trying to study a bit of  everything which is sort of I don't know  if that's totally efficient but at the  same time if you believe you're  well-rounded you don't want to get like  much stronger at one thing win a few  games I was like okay he's good at that  let's not do that anymore I don't really  know what's between me and you know the  truly elite in the world the other guys  in the top 10 or top five if I did maybe  I would be there I don't know so I try  to study a bit of everything but also it  helps that I'm a full time professional  chess but I have the time to do so it's  easier for me to do that than for  someone who has a nine-to-five and a  bunch of kids it's people who have  actual responsibilities and and can't  just study chess all day it's much  harder for them to and so for people  like that in the real world they have to  compartmentalize their time  appropriately well for me I have much  more to devote so I can study a bit of  everything my favourite player to work  with probably the last player actually  played with yesterday I really enjoy  working with my friend Surya Ganguly  he's a very good analyst I've spent some  time in India and I really there's a  there's a very I mean in India is an  enormous place with a ton of different  people in different culture  but I definitely sort of sense an  overarching kind of culture to the way  they approach Ches improvement that I  really really like and I found that  working with Indian players has been  very helpful and Surya is the one I've  worked with the most and she's also the  one who's closest in rating to me we're  we're very close but I'd also like to  point out exactly what happens when I  play with Surya because a lot of people  when they play with their friends you  know they make quick draws and go home  I'm all the two of us we k